module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.engine_slots_written_beside_the_admission_that_bounds_them

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data engine_slots_written_beside_the_admission_that_bounds_them: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  // ONE FACT -- HOW MANY CONVERSATIONS A REPLICA RUNS AT ONCE -- WRITTEN TWICE: ONCE IN THE ENGINE'S
  // BATCH-SLOT FLAG, WHICH RESERVES VRAM PER SLOT, AND ONCE IN THE ROUTER'S PER-UPSTREAM CAP. The two
  // numbers were free to disagree, the disagreement produced no refusal, and the bill arrived as
  // degradation in a third place: the expert cache.

  identity: "engine_slots_written_beside_the_admission_that_bounds_them" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundBelowTheLadder,
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "FIRST RUNG UP: a production route consumes gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile::replica_engine_launch -- the strata-pool launch cfg / unit render, or the engine-side render of gunbc.serving's turn_admission / router_realization / serving_relocation -- so a launched replica's slots are derived from the shape ON A PRODUCTION PATH, not only in witnesses (a declared later change; nothing in production reads the derivation today). NEXT RUNG: the engine's LAUNCHED batch-slot count is read back from the host (the strata-pool unit's cfg.json / ExecStart) and joined at roster admission against the capacity shape's turns -- the number the router row's cap already renders -- and a replica whose engine reserves slots the admission will never fill, or fewer than it will fill, is refused the roster until the two agree; the fleet-truth reader (gunbc.plans.desired_state_follows_the_machine) carries the read, the shape (gunbc.serving.serving_capacity_anchor::serving_capacity_shape) carries the bound"
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: one quantity -- the admitted concurrency of a serving replica -- is written by two hands at two boundaries. The engine's --parallel reserves VRAM per batch slot AT LAUNCH, whether or not a turn ever lands in the slot; the router's per-upstream cap admits turns INTO the replica at runtime. Each number looked local to its own surface -- an engine flag, a router row -- so no author ever saw the pair, and a pair that disagrees produces no refusal at either boundary: the router simply never sends the surplus turns, and the engine simply never gets them back. The cost surfaces in a THIRD place -- on these MoE cards, surplus slots came straight out of the expert cache, because the expert working set is whatever the batch reservations leave.**",

    "SPECIMEN 1, srv3 (2x RTX 2000 Ada, Ampere Altra), read by the operator's session on 2026-11-24 while tuning the fleet by hand. The engine ran from /home/ubuntu/strata-pool/cfg.json with parallel 8 while the pool router (srv1 openresty gunbc-router, hand-managed ~/router/upstreams.json) held that row's cap at 4. Read beside srv4, which ran the same cards, model and stack with no surplus: srv3 held 4,642 experts (8.34 GiB) resident against srv4's 6,867, hit 77% where srv4 hit 91-95%, and decoded about 6.6 tok/s per conversation; the operator set parallel 8 -> 2 and cap 4 -> 2 at 19:56 UTC and read 6,307 experts (11.35 GiB), 91-95% hit and 13.6-15.8 tok/s per conversation afterwards (15.1 in the 21:40-21:55 window). Standing: ad-hoc operator reads, not instruments -- per-stream figures from the engine journal's own 'done: N tokens in T s (R tok/s)' lines averaged over the named windows, expert counts and hit ratios from engine /metrics differenced by the session's /tmp/snap.py and /tmp/delta.py; the same afternoon's second surge-strip trip (~21:57 UTC) rebooted the host, so the re-read obligation on gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile::strata_concurrency_price_observations stands. The figures are the finding's evidence, not its premise: the STATE was writable regardless of what any reading said.",

    "SPECIMEN 2, ken (three Tesla T10, one Strata engine across them, host-e2d4b7), same day, independent card and owner surface. The router row's hand-written cap 4 was the ONLY admission number the fleet carried for it -- the engine had no row at all -- and at 4-way each conversation decoded 1.0 tok/s; after the operator cut the row's cap to 2 at 20:09 UTC the same engine decoded 25.7 (24.5 in the 21:15-21:30 window). When one of the two numbers does not exist, the other silently becomes the engine's batch size by default; when both exist they can disagree the other way -- the srv4 fleet report of 2026-11-21 records the engine's own batch disabling its slot-file endpoint, which is the admission's warm path disappearing behind an engine flag nobody joined.",

    "THE MEASURED SHAPE OF THE PRICE (carried so the class is not built on prose): on these MoE cards concurrent streams gain little AGGREGATE throughput -- the addendum's pair is srv3 at 14.5 tok/s per machine at 4-way against srv4 at 11.2 at 1-way, four streams buying barely one stream's more -- while EACH stream slows several-fold, because concurrent MoE streams need largely DISJOINT experts and each additional stream mostly adds expert misses plus VRAM pressure on the cache every stream reads. Concurrency is therefore not a free multiplier knob: it is a priced operating-point selection, and the price belongs where the number is decided (the capacity shape's turns against the conversation policy's least per-stream rate, gunbc.serving.serving_capacity_anchor::serving_capacity_shape), not discovered where it is paid. The rows carrying these figures with their standing are gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile::strata_concurrency_price_observations; the relayed c1/c4/c8 aggregates beside them are gunbc.serving.t10_pair_strata_bench_receipt::t10_pair_bench_rows.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS AND THE RECOGNITION RULE: for every concurrency--shaped number the fleet carries, name its authority. If two surfaces each hold one -- an engine flag and a router row, a pool config and a capacity shape, a worker count and a queue depth -- and neither is rendered from the other, the pair is this class: each number is locally legitimate, the disagreement is invisible from either side, and the first artifact is always degradation (a slower decode, a colder cache) three layers from the number responsible. The tell that it is THIS class and not a mere stale copy: the two numbers were never one value copied -- they are two DERIVATIONS of one fact, each answerable only within its own surface, which is why no join ever existed to go red.",

    "WHAT THIS ROW IS NOT. It is not gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::two_admission_authorities_keyed_on_different_relations -- there the two authorities could not even be compared (disjoint keys); here the answers are the same kind of number and could have been joined, and the defect is that nothing authored the join because nothing owned it. It is not gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_cap_set_here_surfaces_as_degradation_there, whose defect is a limit with no refusal anywhere; here a refusal existed on one side of the pair (the router cap refused at 4) and the surplus half (the engine reservation) silently ate the cache -- this class is exactly the SECOND number that row's Specimen Two left unowned. It is not gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_capacity_default_sized_for_one_workload_shape_inherited_by_another, whose defect is the DIMENSION a capacity is stated in. And it is not gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::benchmark_warm_capacity_violation_read_as_engine_performance: the measurements quoted here are ad-hoc operator reads with their standing named, not a receipt's standing being read as another kind.",

    "THE REPAIR AS LANDED (2026-11-24, class B3). The capacity shape's turns is the ONE authority for a replica's admitted concurrency: the router row's cap already rendered it (gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy::router_dev_shaped), and the engine's slots now derive from it too -- gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile::replica_engine_launch is the only path from a production replica's launch parameters to a launchable Strata record, it reads the shape, and it REFUSES rather than rendering argv from unread parameters or an unfilled shape. The writable field is GONE from the PRODUCTION record (extdeps.strata.serve_flags::StrataLaunchParameters has no batch-slot field), so a production row cannot spell the disagreement at authoring time -- the bad state is not checked at render there, it has no field to be written in (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder: derive, do not check; gunbc.design_argument::single_authority). The CLASS as a whole sits BELOW THE LADDER on the production path, matching this row's standing: the bridge (extdeps.strata.serve_flags::strata_launch_with_parallel) is public and gates nothing on its slot-count argument; a slot-carrying StrataServeFlags remains directly constructible -- this corpus builds one directly, the cell record srv3_strata_flags with 8; and replica_engine_launch itself has no production consumer yet -- only the witnesses execute it (gunbc.design_argument::consumption: a derivation no production path runs prevents nothing on that path). The first rung up -- a production route consuming the derivation -- and the next -- the host read-back join -- are both declared in this row's trigger_capability as later changes. The benchmark cell KEEPS its hand-written concurrency, because there the number is the experiment's independent variable, not an admission (test.claim.serving.replica_engine_admission_witness pins both: the cell still writes 8; a production launch takes its slots from the shape or refuses).",

    "CEILING AND WHAT STANDS BELOW IT: StructurallyGuaranteed for the modeled replica -- one derivation, no writable second number. CURRENT: below the ladder on the production path. The production RECORD carries no slot field, and the model-side derivation is real and refuses correctly under the witnesses -- but no production route runs it yet: no render path, unit emitter, or router consumes replica_engine_launch (test.claim.serving.replica_engine_admission_witness aside), so on the path where the failure actually happened the class has no guard (gunbc.design_argument::consumption). The first rung up is that production consumer; the next is the fleet-truth read of the launched slot count joined against the shape at roster admission -- srv3's live cfg.json is hand-edited (the operator's own 19:56 edit moved it 8 -> 2), so a host can drift from the row until that join. Both rungs are named in this row's trigger_capability; the witnesses (test.claim.serving.replica_engine_admission_witness) keep the modeled derivation honest meanwhile -- the 4-way state the fleet ran on 2026-11-24 refuses today with ShapeLeastRateBelowPolicyAtConcurrency, and the 2-way operating point derives exactly the 2 slots the hosts now run.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER: the next fleet read that finds a launched engine's batch-slot count and its router row's cap as independent values on any host -- or the fleet-truth join above landing, which closes the host half and would raise this row to its ceiling by construction.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile", decl_name: "replica_engine_launch", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_replica_profile", decl_name: "strata_concurrency_price_observations", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_capacity_anchor", decl_name: "serving_capacity_shape", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_capacity_anchor", decl_name: "serving_conversation_policy", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy", decl_name: "router_dev_shaped", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.strata.serve_flags", decl_name: "StrataLaunchParameters", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.strata_ada_pair_cell", decl_name: "srv3_strata_launch_parameters", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.serving.replica_engine_admission_witness", decl_name: "the_2026_11_24_four_way_state_refuses_the_engine_launch", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.serving.replica_engine_admission_witness", decl_name: "the_two_way_operating_point_derives_two_slots", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ]
}
